// model must inherit from this
249:1. Pregate predicts layer L+1 from layer L's FFN input, not the real L+1 router input (skips L's MoE+shared-expert output, residual, L+1 attention+norm). Recall may be modest; check `recall_at_k_d1` before attributing speedups. LOOKAHEAD=2 is staler.
250:2. `ORDER=before|after` is largely moot for IO start time because async workers are gated by `demand_active_` (`llama-expert-fill.h:444`); `before` only moves the mincore probes/enqueue earlier. Running async chunks still compete with demand IO.
251:3. In-flight async pages look non-resident to `mincore`, so demand re-issues the read (duplicate syscalls; page-lock serialises) and `bytes_read`/`sync_bytes` include bytes the async side also read. `hit_inflight` experts therefore are not a clean "resident before demand".
255:7. `DIAG=1` observes every MUL_MAT_ID (`:1144-1149`) -> extra cuts + syscalls; DIAG timing is not comparable to non-DIAG timing. `majflt_*` use RUSAGE_SELF (whole process, including CUDA/other threads), not just expert-fault attribution.
256:8. PREGATE=1 with FILL=0 is silently inactive (init is inside `expert_fill_init`); `expert_config: effective ... pregate=1` is printed though pregate is not active. Engagement check `expert_pregate: active` guards this.
258:10. `LLAMA_EXPERT_FILL_RANDOM=1` adds expert ranges to the lazy-range list, which also turns off MAP_POPULATE for the whole file (`llama-mmap.cpp:509`), a side effect on non-expert pages; `decode` variant avoids it but applies MADV_RANDOM only from first decode token (prompt-phase mmap faults, if any, unaffected, and MAX_TOKENS=1 means no fill in prompt).
260:12. `expert_demand_tokens` counts filled-ubatch tokens only; with MIN/MAX changes `demand_wait_ns_per_token` denominators change meaning.
261:13. Fill/pregate cost per layer on the critical path (mincore probes, scalar router dot, fill job build) scales with n_expert*n_embd and K; no timers cover it.

